      {
        "id": "p21-q4a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "How many solutions to $\\sin x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}$ are there in this domain? Explain how you know from the graphs.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "4 solutions, because the graphs have 4 points of intersection.",
          "worked": "Each intersection of $y=\\sin x$ and $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}$ gives a solution. There are four intersections in $-2\\pi\\le x\\le2\\pi$, so there are 4 solutions.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q4b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the exact solutions of $x$ in this domain.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=-\\frac{7\\pi}{4},\\,-\\frac{5\\pi}{4},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi}{4}$",
          "worked": "The reference angle is $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$. Sine is positive in quadrants I and II.\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\qquad(0\\le x<2\\pi).\\end{align*}$$\nSubtract $2\\pi$ to obtain the two negative solutions:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{\\pi}{4}-2\\pi&=-\\frac{7\\pi}{4}\\\\\\frac{3\\pi}{4}-2\\pi&=-\\frac{5\\pi}{4}.\\end{align*}$$\nThus, in the given domain,\n$$x=-\\frac{7\\pi}{4},\\,-\\frac{5\\pi}{4},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi}{4}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      }
    ],
